# Corpora this project vendors but does not author.
#
# Path exclusion is the narrowest of the three dispositions and is reserved for
# material that cannot be reworded: an immutable record, a self-referential test
# corpus, or third-party source carried verbatim. These are the third kind. They
# are upstream's files, synced rather than edited, and rewording a numeral in
# them would diverge from upstream for no gain and conflict on the next sync.
#
# Validated before excluding, as the rule requires: a full scan of all tracked
# files reports zero blocking flags, so nothing real is hidden in here. Every
# finding in these paths is a bare-numeral warning on ordinary arithmetic or on
# a kernel or compiler version in a comment.

# libev, embedded upstream event loop (Marc Lehmann, BSD-2-Clause / GPL-2.0+)
libev/

# nanobench, single-header microbenchmark library (Martin Ankerl, MIT)
bench/nanobench.h

# Intel's licence text, carried byte for byte beside the emulator it covers.
# Not UTF-8, so the scanner cannot read it at all, and it must not be re-encoded
# any more than it may be reworded.
bench/SDE-LICENSE.txt

# The allowlist itself. Every entry is by definition a phrase carrying a
# tell-shaped token, so scanning this file re-reports each declaration as a
# finding. This is the self-referential case the rule already names: material
# that must embed the shapes the tool detects is excluded through the ignore
# mechanism rather than reworded into uselessness. Its integrity is checked by
# host-lint lexicon --check, which is the gate that actually applies here.
LEXICON
